<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Danish West India and Guinea Company</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1671">1671</a>–<a href="/years/1776">1776</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Danish West India Company</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Danish West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a> Company was a Danish chartered company founded in 1671 to run colonies in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> and trade on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-coast">Gold Coast</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>. It administered St Thomas, St John and, from 1733, St Croix, bought from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, as well as forts such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osu-castle">Christiansborg</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/accra">Accra</a>. It shipped enslaved Africans to its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> plantations until the crown took over its possessions in 1754–1776.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/danish-west-india-and-guinea-company.jpg" alt="Danish West India and Guinea Company" width="320" height="238" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vestindisk-guineisk_Kompagnis_flag.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The company grew out of the Danish Africa Company, founded at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gluckstadt">Glückstadt</a> in March 1659 by the originally Finnish Hendrik Carloff, the Dutchmen Isaac Coymans and Nicolaes Pancras, and the German merchants Vincent Klingenberg and Jacob del Boe, to trade with the Danish Gold Coast in present-day Ghana. The Danish West India Company was organised on 20 November 1670 and chartered by King Christian V on 11 March 1671, absorbing the Africa Company. From 30 August 1680 it was known as the West India–Guinea Company.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a> had settled on St Thomas in 1668, using ships of the Royal Dano-Norwegian Navy such as the yacht Den forgyldte Krone and the frigate Færøe, but the company soon sailed ships of its own, among them the Charlotte Amelie and the Young Tobias, and relied on the navy mainly for escort. At first it struggled to make a profit; it raised its revenue by increasing taxes and bringing all colonial exports directly to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a>. It bought St John in 1718.</p><p>In the 17th and 18th centuries the company prospered on the triangular trade of the North Atlantic, in which enslaved people from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-coast">Gold Coast</a> were exchanged for molasses and rum in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-indies">West Indies</a>. An estimated 120,000 enslaved Africans were carried on its ships. After 1754 the colonies were governed by the government's Chamber of Revenues, from 1760 to 1848 under the name Vestindisk-guineiske rente- og generaltoldkammer.</p><p>On 18 March 1765 a royal resolution allowed Frederik Bargum to revive the trade as the Guinea Company, which in November received the forts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osu-castle">Christiansborg</a> and Fredensborg for 20 years. Unlike the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-west-india-company">Dutch West India Company</a> it never held a monopoly, and Danish, Norwegian, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleswig">Schleswig</a> and Holstein firms competed with it. Bargum fled his creditors in 1774, the government took back the forts in August and September 1775, and the company was liquidated on 22 November 1776.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Danish West India Company” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Danish West India and Guinea Company</b> and later the crown ran a string of forts east of Accra, exchanging textiles, guns and alcohol for gold and enslaved Africans, who were shipped to the Danish West Indies.</blockquote><a href="/state/danish-gold-coast">Danish Gold Coast</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Danish West India Company</b> settled St Thomas in 1672 and St John in 1718, and bought St Croix from France in 1733.</blockquote><a href="/state/danish-west-indies">Danish West Indies</a></li><li><blockquote>France sold it to the <b>Danish West India and Guinea Company</b> in 1733, after which it became the sugar centre of the Danish West Indies, later part of the United States Virgin Islands.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-saint-croix">French Saint Croix</a></li></ul></section>
